WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:05.432
Compressed air is clean and handy, which is why it is everywhere: in cylinders,

2
00:00:05.665 --> 00:00:07.778
valves, tools and blow guns.

3
00:00:08.483 --> 00:00:11.792
It is also the most expensive way to deliver energy.

4
00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:14.494
A motor drives the compressor,

5
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.220
and it squeezes outside air to around 7 bar above atmospheric pressure.

6
00:00:20.588 --> 00:00:24.338
Of the electricity the compressor takes in, most becomes heat.

7
00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:29.119
Only around 10 percent does useful work at the point of use.

8
00:00:30.128 --> 00:00:33.657
Heat recovery wins part of it back through a heat exchanger,

9
00:00:33.774 --> 00:00:35.469
for hot water or space heating.

10
00:00:36.807 --> 00:00:41.231
Outside air holds water vapour, and after compression that water condenses.

11
00:00:41.953 --> 00:00:44.728
Water and oil damage valves and cylinders.

12
00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:50.408
That is why a dryer stands after the compressor, to take the water out of the air.

13
00:00:51.126 --> 00:00:54.434
Without a dryer, condensate collects in the pipes.

14
00:00:55.455 --> 00:01:00.006
Filters take out oil and dust, and a drain lets out the water they catch.

15
00:01:01.326 --> 00:01:07.351
Before each machine sits an FRL unit: filter, regulator and lubricator in a row.

16
00:01:08.353 --> 00:01:12.405
The filter catches dirt and water once more, just before the machine.

17
00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:17.625
The regulator sets the pressure this machine needs, here 6 bar,

18
00:01:17.892 --> 00:01:19.715
even when the network is higher.

19
00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:21.971
The gauge shows that pressure.

20
00:01:23.017 --> 00:01:26.767
The lubricator adds an oil mist where components ask for it.

21
00:01:27.557 --> 00:01:30.401
Many modern cylinders and valves do without.

22
00:01:31.751 --> 00:01:35.327
The network pressure is the pressure the compressors keep in the pipes.

23
00:01:36.034 --> 00:01:39.377
Each extra bar costs roughly 7 percent more energy;

24
00:01:39.575 --> 00:01:42.199
estimates run from 6 to 8 percent.

25
00:01:43.237 --> 00:01:46.186
The higher the pressure, the harder every leak blows.

26
00:01:47.224 --> 00:01:51.403
Many plants run at 7 or 8 bar, because one machine needs it.

27
00:01:52.205 --> 00:01:54.028
Then the whole plant pays for it.

28
00:01:55.053 --> 00:01:58.977
Solve that machine locally: with a booster or a small extra compressor,

29
00:01:59.105 --> 00:02:01.589
a bigger supply line or a tank of its own.

30
00:02:02.342 --> 00:02:04.513
Then the whole network can come down.

31
00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:07.241
Leaks are the big loss.

32
00:02:07.981 --> 00:02:12.508
Without a leak programme, often 20 to 30 percent of the air escapes,

33
00:02:12.695 --> 00:02:14.889
through couplings, hoses and valves.

34
00:02:15.905 --> 00:02:19.794
The leak test shows it: at the weekend, with production stopped,

35
00:02:19.992 --> 00:02:24.473
check how much of the time the compressor still runs loaded, actually compressing.

36
00:02:25.272 --> 00:02:27.002
That share is mostly leaks.

37
00:02:27.992 --> 00:02:28.828
An example:

38
00:02:28.956 --> 00:02:33.867
this 75 kilowatt compressor delivers 10 cubic metres a minute when loaded.

39
00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:38.916
Nothing should use air now, but it runs loaded 40 percent of the time.

40
00:02:39.963 --> 00:02:44.700
Then about 40 percent of 10, so 4 cubic metres a minute, leaks away.

41
00:02:45.391 --> 00:02:46.366
Day and night.

42
00:02:47.722 --> 00:02:48.929
Leaks run all year.

43
00:02:49.621 --> 00:02:53.383
Take 75 kilowatts when loaded, times 40 percent,

44
00:02:53.557 --> 00:03:00.117
times 8,760 hours: about 263,000 kilowatt hours.

45
00:03:01.143 --> 00:03:06.390
At fifteen cents per kilowatt hour, that is roughly 39,000 euros a year,

46
00:03:06.646 --> 00:03:11.011
ignoring the power it draws running unloaded, idling without compressing.

47
00:03:11.996 --> 00:03:16.198
You find leaks with an ultrasonic detector, which hears the hiss you can’t.

48
00:03:16.998 --> 00:03:19.343
Each leak gets a tag and is repaired.

49
00:03:20.392 --> 00:03:25.117
A round like that typically costs a few thousand euros: a classic quick win.

50
00:03:25.863 --> 00:03:30.716
Then lower the network pressure by 1 bar, and save roughly another 7 percent.

51
00:03:32.092 --> 00:03:36.724
The third loss is inappropriate use: an open blow-off that blows all day,

52
00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:39.859
cooling with air, or making vacuum with compressed air.

53
00:03:40.856 --> 00:03:45.128
A fan, blower or vacuum pump does that job for a fraction of the energy.

54
00:03:46.101 --> 00:03:49.015
And never blow compressed air at your skin or clothes:

55
00:03:49.142 --> 00:03:51.581
air can get under the skin through a small cut,

56
00:03:51.755 --> 00:03:53.357
and dirt into your eyes.

57
00:03:54.396 --> 00:03:58.913
What first: more efficient compressors with variable speed, or less demand?

58
00:03:59.691 --> 00:04:03.430
Experts argue about it, but cutting demand is usually cheaper.

59
00:04:04.192 --> 00:04:06.828
So ask on every walk: what is this air used for,

60
00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.091
and what could do it without compressed air?
